WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:06.400
In 1927, 29 of the top physicists gathered at&nbsp;
the prestigious Solvay Conference in Brussels.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:09.520
The only woman in attendance was Marie Curie.

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:10.880
She had a lot of firsts.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:13.440
She was the first woman to win a Nobel Prize.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:16.160
The first person to win the Nobel Prize twice.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:19.280
And the first person to win&nbsp;
in two different fields.

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:22.560
Curie is best known for her work&nbsp;
in radioactivity which would save&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.960
a million lives during the first world war.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.200
But would ultimately take her own.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:30.800
Marie Curie was born Maria&nbsp;
Salomea Skłodowska in Warsaw,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:36.400
Poland then under the control of the&nbsp;
Russian Empire on November 7, 1867.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:38.480
She was the youngest child of teachers.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:40.320
Her mother, Bronisława Skłodowska,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:43.360
was the headteacher of a prestigious&nbsp;
boarding school for girls.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:48.400
Her father, Wladyslaw Sklodowski, taught physics&nbsp;
and math and was proud of his Polish heritage.

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:52.080
As a result of his patriotism, his&nbsp;
Russian supervisors forced him into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:56.160
lower-paying positions. He also lost&nbsp;
his savings through a bad investment.

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:01.600
To support their five children, they had to&nbsp;
take in student boarders. This would be fatal.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:06.960
Maria’s eldest sibling, her sister Zofia,&nbsp;
caught typhus from one of the lodgers and died.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.200
A few years later, when Maria was&nbsp;
ten, her mother died of tuberculosis.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:16.400
The tragedies caused Maria to give up Catholicism&nbsp;
- the faith of her mom - and become agnostic.

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:19.840
Her father wouldn’t forgive himself&nbsp;
for losing his family’s savings.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:22.480
However, his children would&nbsp;
remember him as the man who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:25.360
nurtured them emotionally and intellectually.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:27.280
Maria finished high school at the top of her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:31.120
class but wasn’t allowed to attend&nbsp;
university because she was a woman.

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:35.040
The Russian empire banned women&nbsp;
from getting a university education.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:39.280
So she and her sister Bronisława (or&nbsp;
Bronya for short) enrolled in the secretive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:43.840
Flying University - or Floating University&nbsp;
- in Warsaw - named after the ever-changing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:47.920
location of classes to avoid the&nbsp;
watchful eye of Czarist authorities.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:50.720
Her sister then left for medical school in Paris.

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:54.880
Maria hoped to eventually join her. The&nbsp;
two made a pact: Maria would support her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:58.880
sister’s studies in Paris, and Bronya&nbsp;
would return the favor in the future.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:01.920
So, from the age of 17,&nbsp;
Maria worked as a governess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:04.160
tutor, and also studied in her spare time.

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:06.720
While working for relatives, the Żorawskis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:10.480
she fell in love with their son, Kazimierz,&nbsp;
who would become a mathematician.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:14.800
But the Żorawskis didn’t approve of her&nbsp;
because she didn’t have a penny to her name.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:18.320
It was said that as an old man,&nbsp;
Kazimierz would sit contemplatively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:21.840
before the statue of her in front of the&nbsp;
research institute she went on to found.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:28.160
In 1891, when Maria was 24, she finally&nbsp;
had the means to join her sister in Paris,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:30.160
and now used the name Marie.

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:32.400
She enrolled at the University of Paris - known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:35.520
as the Sorbonne - where she&nbsp;
studied physics and mathematics.

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:38.640
At first, she lived in the home&nbsp;
of her sister who was now married&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:42.080
but later opted to rent a little&nbsp;
attic closer to the university.

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:44.400
She often stayed at the heated library until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:47.520
closing rather than spend the&nbsp;
evening in her unheated room.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:51.680
Her earlier education had been insufficient&nbsp;
so there was a lot of catching up to do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:55.600
She sometimes worked so hard she&nbsp;
forgot to eat and would pass out.

00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:00.080
Despite the difficulties, Marie marveled&nbsp;
at her freedom, writing: “It was like a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:05.360
world opened to me, the world of science, which&nbsp;
I was at last permitted to know in all liberty.”

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:08.560
She earned a degree in physics,&nbsp;
and then another in mathematics.&nbsp;

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:13.280
She planned on returning to Poland but&nbsp;
then Pierre Curie came into her life.

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:16.000
He was eight years older, a well-known physicist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:19.120
and an outsider who was educated&nbsp;
by his father in his teens.

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:23.200
They were introduced by a mutual friend who&nbsp;
knew Marie needed lab space for her research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:26.160
and Pierre headed a laboratory at&nbsp;
the School of Industrial Physics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:28.480
and Chemistry where engineers were trained.

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:32.560
Marie would say of Pierre: “He had&nbsp;
dedicated his life to his dream of science:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:36.240
he felt the need of a companion&nbsp;
who could live his dream with him.”

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:38.800
And he hoped that companion would be her.

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:43.280
But Marie turned down his marriage proposal since&nbsp;
her plan was to return to her native country.

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:47.200
However, she learned that it wouldn’t&nbsp;
be possible to start a career in Poland.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:51.120
When she went back to visit her&nbsp;
family during summer break in 1894,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:55.280
Krakow University denied her a job as&nbsp;
a professor because she was a woman.

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:58.800
Pierre convinced her to come&nbsp;
back to Paris to pursue a PhD.

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:03.680
She insisted that he, too, get his doctorate,&nbsp;
which he did, pioneering research on magnetism.

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:08.000
They married in 1895 at the town hall&nbsp;
in Sceaux in the suburbs of Paris.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:10.320
Partners in life and in science.

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:15.200
Marie wore a dark blue outfit on her wedding day&nbsp;
that would become her trademark in the laboratory.

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:17.040
They bought bicycles with the money they received&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:21.840
as a wedding gift - their way of relaxing&nbsp;
in a life otherwise filled with research.

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:26.640
Marie Curie would earn her Doctor of&nbsp;
Science degree from the Sorbonne in 1903.

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:32.160
She did her thesis on radiation, which was&nbsp;
recently discovered in uranium by Henri Becquerel.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:36.080
Curie was intrigued by Becquerel’s&nbsp;
discovery and investigated further.
 &nbsp;

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:39.760
She used an electrometer invented by&nbsp;
her husband and his brother to measure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:42.960
radioactivity in many substances and minerals.

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:44.880
She realized through her experiments that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:48.080
radiation was a property&nbsp;
of the element of uranium.

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:51.360
Yet when she observed the mineral&nbsp;
pitchblende which primarily contains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:55.840
uranium - she noticed it was far more&nbsp;
radioactive than uranium could explain.

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:57.600
How could this be?

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:01.680
It would only be possible if there&nbsp;
were something else in the pitchblende.

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:05.920
Pierre was so intrigued that he dropped&nbsp;
his own work to join her in her search.

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:10.000
They ground up tons of pitchblende&nbsp;
and discovered an element that was 400&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:15.520
times more radioactive than uranium.&nbsp;
Polonium. Named after her country of birth.

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:20.960
And then, they discovered another element that&nbsp;
gave off 900 times more radiation than polonium:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:21.920
radium.

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:26.000
The unglamorous work of extracting and&nbsp;
isolating the elements took place in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:30.880
leaky and drafty shack near Pierre’s work&nbsp;
as they didn’t have a dedicated lab space.

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:32.560
Their efforts paid off.

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:36.560
The Nobel Prize in Physics in&nbsp;
1903 went to Marie, Pierre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:39.120
and Becquerel for their research in radiation.

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:43.840
French academics originally proposed that&nbsp;
only Pierre and Becquerel receive the prize.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:46.320
Leaving Marie out. A sign of the times.

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:49.200
However, a sympathetic member&nbsp;
of the nominating committee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:53.760
Swedish mathematician Gösta Mittage-Leffler&nbsp;
alerted Pierre to the situation.

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:56.240
He insisted that his wife share the honor.

00:05:56.240 --> 00:06:00.080
Marie Curie became the first&nbsp;
woman to win a Nobel Prize.

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:02.480
She and her husband were too&nbsp;
busy with their research to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:04.880
accept the award in person in Stockholm.

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:08.640
Pierre was also sick, suffering&nbsp;
from pain and fatigue.

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:13.360
They had no idea at the time that radiation&nbsp;
could be detrimental to their health.

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:17.520
It is said that Marie would carry tubes of&nbsp;
radium in her pockets. She was fascinated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:23.520
by what she described as "faint fairy lights".&nbsp;
Little did she know this was slowly killing her.

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:26.480
The glowing green radium captivated the public.

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:29.200
It would be a key element&nbsp;
in early cancer treatment.

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:33.600
And would also find its way into everyday&nbsp;
products: toothpaste with a promise of benefitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:38.560
teeth and facial creams in the belief that it&nbsp;
would firm muscles and smooth out wrinkles.

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:41.360
The element was so popular that in the 1920s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:47.040
a single gram of radium cost more than&nbsp;
$100,000 - well over a million today!

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:51.280
The Curies could have tried to patent radium&nbsp;
and cash in big time, but they didn’t.

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:55.760
Marie declared: “Radium is a chemical&nbsp;
element, a property of all humans.”

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:57.840
After their ground-breaking work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:01.920
it was Pierre who would be promoted as head&nbsp;
of the physics department at the Sorbonne.

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:04.160
Yet he still didn’t have a proper lab.

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:06.560
Pierre complained and the university relented,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:10.640
however, he would never get his dream&nbsp;
of working in a new laboratory because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:14.400
tragedy struck less than two years after&nbsp;
the birth of their second daughter.

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:19.840
On a rainy day in April 1906,&nbsp;
Pierre was walking across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:24.720
Rue Dauphine when he was run over by a&nbsp;
horse and carriage. He died instantly.

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:27.440
Pierre’s father implied that&nbsp;
his son’s preoccupation with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:29.920
his own thoughts contributed to his death.

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:34.480
Marie was offered his academic post at the&nbsp;
Sorbonne instead of accepting a widow’s pension.

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:37.520
She became the first female professor in France.

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:42.320
Hundreds of people lined up outside the&nbsp;
university hoping to attend her first lecture.

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:46.640
The period following her husband’s death&nbsp;
would be the most difficult of her life.

00:07:46.640 --> 00:07:51.360
In 1911, the French Academy of Sciences, the&nbsp;
gathering place for prominent scientists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:55.520
rejected her for membership when she&nbsp;
put herself forward for a vacant seat.

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:59.280
They passed her over for physicist&nbsp;
and inventor Edouard Branly.

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:02.720
Many suspected it was because&nbsp;
she was an immigrant and a woman.

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:07.920
Despite getting snubbed by the Academy, she went&nbsp;
on to win something even greater later that year.

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:13.120
A second Nobel Prize - this time in chemistry,&nbsp;
for the discovery of polonium and radium,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:17.360
the isolation of radium, and the study&nbsp;
of the nature of that remarkable element.

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:19.440
But the buzz around her wasn’t great.

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:23.200
The French press was all over her affair&nbsp;
with her husband’s former student,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:26.960
physician Paul Langevin, who was&nbsp;
married but estranged from his wife.

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:29.760
She was labeled as a homewrecker&nbsp;
and even warned that it might be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:32.400
best if she didn’t pick up the Prize in person.

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:34.880
Curie sank into a deep depression.

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:38.720
Only to be slowly pulled out with&nbsp;
the support of a fellow scientist.

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:43.760
Albert Einstein struck up a friendship with&nbsp;
Curie at the Solvay conference in 1911.

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:47.360
He wrote her a letter of&nbsp;
encouragement during this dark period.

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:50.800
“I am impelled to tell you how much&nbsp;
I have come to admire your intellect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:52.880
your drive, and your honesty...”

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:58.240
He then told her to pay no mind to the stories&nbsp;
in the press: “..simply don’t read that hogwash…”

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:01.440
Curie went to Stockholm to&nbsp;
accept her second Nobel Prize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:04.400
and the headlines about the&nbsp;
affair eventually blew over.

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:08.160
She would slowly recover and was in the&nbsp;
middle of setting up a giant laboratory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:11.920
at her newly created Radium&nbsp;
Institute when war broke out.

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:16.000
As German troops headed toward Paris,&nbsp;
she took her stash of precious radium&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:20.240
to a bank vault in Bordeaux, in&nbsp;
southwestern France, the new capital.

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:22.560
She also tried to sell her two gold Nobel prize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:26.560
medals to help the war effort but the&nbsp;
national bank refused to accept them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:31.520
She would buy war bonds using her prize money&nbsp;
but this self-sacrifice wasn’t enough for her.

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:35.760
She was determined to use her research&nbsp;
to save the lives of French soldiers.

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:40.320
She had studied the work of German scientist&nbsp;
Wilhelm Roentgen who had discovered x-rays.

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:44.320
Curie then brought x-ray machines to the&nbsp;
battlefield by inventing mobile units&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.320 --> 00:09:49.840
called “little curies” to help surgeons locate and&nbsp;
remove shrapnel and bullets from wounded soldiers.

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:53.760
She and her daughter Irène trained&nbsp;
150 women to drive these little cars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:56.080
and drove one herself, despite the danger.

00:09:56.080 --> 00:10:00.320
Curie also oversaw 200 radiological&nbsp;
rooms in field hospitals.

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:02.320
It’s estimated that by the end of the war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.320 --> 00:10:07.120
her efforts saved the lives of a million&nbsp;
men but may have cost her her own.

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:10.880
Curie knew that over-exposure to&nbsp;
x-rays would pose risks to her health.

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:14.080
But there wasn’t any time to&nbsp;
improve on safety practices.

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:18.400
Years later, she would die of aplastic&nbsp;
anaemia - a blood disease likely due to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:21.840
exposure to large amounts of&nbsp;
radiation over her lifetime.

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:25.120
Despite her humanitarian efforts,&nbsp;
the French government never gave her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:29.440
any official recognition whereas she&nbsp;
was gaining increasing fame abroad.

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:32.720
In 1921, U.S. President Warren Harding invited her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:36.800
to the White House and gave her a gift of&nbsp;
a gram of radium to aid in her research.

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:41.040
The French government was apparently embarrassed&nbsp;
by the fact that they gave her no distinctions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:44.960
so, before that trip to DC, they offered&nbsp;
her the country’s most distinguished honor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:47.760
the Légion d’Honneur - the Legion of Honor.

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:49.040
She declined.

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:54.240
During her later years, she headed the Radium&nbsp;
Institute - later the Curie Institute in Paris.

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:58.160
And opened another in Warsaw, where&nbsp;
her sister Bronya became the director.

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:01.520
Both remain major research&nbsp;
institutions to this day.

00:11:01.520 --> 00:11:03.760
She was already in ill health by then.

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:12.240
On July 4, 1934, Curie died at the age of 66 at a&nbsp;
sanatorium in the town of Passy in eastern France.

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:16.240
She didn’t live to see her daughter Irène&nbsp;
win her own Nobel Prize in Chemistry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:20.240
a year later for the artificial&nbsp;
creation of new radioactive elements,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:23.760
sharing it with her husband,&nbsp;
physicist Frédéric Joliot.

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:28.000
Curie was buried at a cemetery in Sceaux,&nbsp;
the suburbs of Paris where she married,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:29.520
and where her husband lay.

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:32.960
In 1995, both were moved to the Panthéon in Paris,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:39.120
the resting place for many distinguished French&nbsp;
citizens like Victor Hugo, Rousseau, and Voltaire.

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:42.960
Curie was the first woman to be honored&nbsp;
in the Panthéon on her own merits.

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:44.720
Her remains remained radioactive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.720 --> 00:11:48.160
so they were placed in a coffin&nbsp;
lined with nearly an inch of lead.

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:50.720
Even her papers are still radioactive today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.720 --> 00:11:54.640
Anyone who wants to examine them must&nbsp;
wear protective gear and sign a waiver.

00:11:54.640 --> 00:11:58.240
Curie’s tireless work was surpassed&nbsp;
only by her fight to overcome the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:02.720
barriers in her way to become one of&nbsp;
the greatest scientists of all time.

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:06.400
It wasn’t only her work that was&nbsp;
impressive but also her work ethic.

